+# SMS authentication integration
+
+This repository is a static homepage. The form calls the following same-origin backend endpoints; it does not send SMS or authenticate locally, and never accepts an arbitrary six-digit code.
+
+## Request a code
+
+POST /api/auth/sms/request
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+Request: { "phone": "+12025550123", "purpose": "rewards" }
+Response: { "challengeId": "opaque-server-issued-id" }
+
+Purpose is either "rewards" or "order". Return successful JSON only after the SMS provider accepts the message. Send this format, replacing the hostname with the exact production hostname (no protocol, port, or path), and the example code with a securely generated six-digit code:
+
+Your Daily Grind sign-in code is 012345.
+
+@your-production-hostname #012345
+
+The origin-bound line must be the last line of the message.
+
+## Verify a code
+
+POST /api/auth/sms/verify
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+Request: { "challengeId": "opaque-server-issued-id", "phone": "+12025550123", "code": "012345", "purpose": "rewards" }
+Response: { "verified": true }
+
+Validate the challenge, phone, purpose, expiry, and code on the server. Consume a successful challenge once, invalidate earlier codes when resending, cap verification attempts, and rate-limit requests. Use a short code expiry and store only a protected code hash. Do not log codes or expose whether a phone has an account. Establish the authenticated session with a Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite cookie before returning success. Enforce same-origin/CSRF protection for JSON POST requests and HTTPS in production.
+
+The client navigates to /rewards or /order-online only on explicit verified: true. These routes must enforce the server session. It does not trust redirects supplied by an API response.
+
+Return JSON with HTTP 400/401/410 for rejected or expired challenges, 429 for rate limits, and 5xx for service errors. The UI provides safe local messages for these responses, network failure, and a missing backend.
+
+## Browser behavior
+
+The single code field supports manual input, paste, leading zeroes, numeric keyboards, and autocomplete="one-time-code". In secure contexts with OTPCredential support, the form listens before requesting an SMS and submits extracted codes through requestSubmit(). Listening ends on manual submission, resend, phone/purpose changes, page exit, or a two-minute timeout. Permission denial preserves manual entry. Resending has a client-side 30-second cooldown; rate limits must also be enforced on the server.
